    <h3>Introduction</h3>
      <p>Forest fires in the Amazon cause vegetation cover loss and threaten biodiversity. The disturbances caused by fires on the carbon and hydrological cycles influence the global climate and generate uncertainties in future climate projections (Baker et al., 2009). Most forest fires in the Amazon are directly associated with anthropogenic actions, such as deforestation or agricultural practices (Tang & Arellano, 2017). In the past decades, the years with the highest frequency and severity of forest fires coincided with the warm phase of <b>El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO)</b> and correlated with a decrease in precipitation in the Amazon basin (Ropelewski & Halpert, 1987; Taufik et al., 2017). The abnormal warming of <b>sea surface temperatures (SST)</b> in the North Atlantic causes a northward shift of the <b>Intertropical Convergence Zone (ITCZ)</b>, reducing rainfall in the west and south of the Amazon basin (Yoon & Zeng, 2010). For instance, there is a high correlation between the cumulative number of fires during the dry season and the increase in SST before the fire season (Chen et al., 2011). Thus, the extension and intensity of forest fires can be affected by climatic oscillations.</p>
      <p>Droughts in the hydrological cycle are defined by a decline in precipitation that causes a deficit of soil moisture, followed by a decrease in streamflow and groundwater levels (Changnon, 1987). Droughts can be classified according to their effect on the hydrological cycle. <b>Meteorological drought</b> is the deficiency of precipitation in a region during a specific period (Belal et al., 2014), while <b>Hydrological Drought</b> refers to the deficits of accessible water in surface water storage and groundwater reservoirs relative to standard conditions (Gerdener et al., 2020). Therefore, while groundwater levels in unconfined or semi-confined aquifers indicate meteorological conditions that occur on timescales from weeks to years, near-surface water storage corresponds to the amount of precipitation (Terada et al., 2022). Drought monitoring is generally based on in-situ measurements of precipitation or river levels (Chen et al., 2010). Other indicators of drought intensity, such as groundwater storage measurements, are not always incorporated without direct measurements in space and time (Thomas et al., 2017). Thus, measurements of meteorological and hydrological droughts based on information obtained from remote sensing are needed to understand drought events.
      </p>
        <p>The characterization of hydrological drought can be done using satellite observations of the <b>Gravity Recovery and Climate Experiment (GRACE)</b> mission (Tapley et al., 2019), which allows the detection of <b>Terrestrial Water Storage (TWS)</b>, integrating soil moisture, snow water equivalent, surface water from vegetation cover, and groundwater storage (Yin et al., 2020). However, the information generated by GRACE can only be separated into individual hydrological components through auxiliary data (Zheng et al., 2012). Based on the Catchment Land Surface Model, the GRACE Data Assimilation System improves the value of TWS data by allowing space-time scaling and vertical decomposition of the moisture components. Thus, it is possible to address hydrological drought by using the <b>Drought Indicators (DI)</b> for <b>surface soil moisture (sfsm)</b>, <b>root zone soil moisture (rtzsm)</b>, and <b>groundwater storage (gws)</b>. The current humidity conditions in each of these soil zones are expressed as the probability of occurrence of drought events, with low drought indicators defined as soil moisture drier than average values, and high values indicating soil moisture wetter than average (Houborg et al., 2012). The use of the DI provides information on the severity of the drought in each site and can contribute to the distinction of hydrological drought based on subsoil depth (Nie et al., 2018).
        </p>
      <p>The characterization of meteorological drought can be done by using the <b>standardized precipitation index (SPI)</b> (McKee et al., 1993) since it can be calculated on different timescales and depends solely on precipitation obtained from the <b>Tropical Rainfall Measuring Mission (TRMM)</b>, without the need for auxiliary variables. Furthermore, SPI evaluated with short timescales of 1 month is also related to soil moisture (Wang et al., 2019).
      </p><br><br>

      <h3>Methods to monitoring Drought and Wildfires</h3>
      <p>To investigate the impact of groundwater and El Niño events on forest fires in the Amazon, this study employed a comprehensive approach that utilized various data sources and methods (Fig. 1). The study area covered the Amazon River basin, the largest drainage basin on the planet, and spanned from 2004 to 2016. The hydrological drought was characterized by using drought indicators (DI) at different soil depths, derived from the GRACE Data Assimilation System. The meteorological drought was assessed by using the standardized precipitation index (SPI), based on the TRMM precipitation data. The occurrence and extent of forest fires were obtained from the GFED4 database, which provides the monthly <b>burned fraction area (bf)</b>. The relationship between drought and fire was analyzed by using correlation, linear regression, and ANOVA methods. The influence of El Niño-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) events on drought and fire was also explored by using the <b>Oceanic Niño Index (ONI)</b>. The data processing, analytical survey and visualizations were performed in the statistical computer language R.</p><br><br>
      
